nal,' the ex-enforcer Cole said. Below, the Patrol's lights converged like angry stars. Dane Kim stood at the window with a data chip and thought of green things growing."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Remembering', 'text': "Dane Kim and the ex-enforcer Cole were in the tower when the door blew. The ex-enforcer held the stairs long enough. Long enough for what? For the footage to loop. For the memory to root. For the Overseer's lie to die on every screen at once.\n\nBy dawn the Patrol had stood down — human, after all, and shown the same thirty seconds. the Overseer was gone by noon, the Algorithm's servers dark. New Babel belonged to its people again. It had only ever been on loan.\n\na data chip burned out in the broadcast — one use, that was the price. Dane Kim held the dead chip and didn't mourn it. Seeds die to grow. That was the whole point."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'After the Static', 'text': "Rebuilding New Babel was harder than freeing it. Dane Kim organized food lines with the same care once used for heists. the ex-enforcer Cole taught kids to code on salvaged terminals. The medic ran a clinic. The runner ran messages of hope.\n\nThe footage from a data chip became New Babel's founding document: not a constitution, but a memory. This is what we were. This is what we can be. memory is resistance — carved, at the ex-enforcer Cole's insistence, over the tower door.\n\nDane Kim kept the dead chip in a pocket. A reminder: that truth is a seed, that seeds die to grow, that one a data chip in the right hands at the right time is worth an army."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'The Habit of Freedom', 'text': "memory is resistance — Dane Kim thinks it every time, like a prayer, like a promise. Memory is resistance. Freedom is a habit. Practice daily.\n\nAnd somewhere, in a gutter where drones don't look, someone is finding something warm that shouldn't be warm. It starts again. It always starts again. That's the best part.\n\nSometimes Dane Kim walks to the tower and looks out over New